1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:02,630
Winter in the Arctic.

2
00:00:02,880 --> 00:00:05,010
The sun hasn't risen for weeks.

3
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:08,450
Outside, the air can drop to minus fifty.

4
00:00:08,960 --> 00:00:11,990
Inside the apartment
blocks, it's plus twenty.

5
00:00:12,360 --> 00:00:16,350
And under every one of those
blocks, the ground is frozen solid.

6
00:00:17,757 --> 00:00:19,587
This is Norilsk.

7
00:00:19,777 --> 00:00:22,687
One hundred and seventy-four
thousand people,

8
00:00:22,837 --> 00:00:26,247
three hundred kilometers
north of the Arctic Circle.

9
00:00:26,777 --> 00:00:29,987
No road leads here from
the rest of the country.

10
00:00:31,330 --> 00:00:34,810
In twenty twenty, one giant
fuel tank here failed,

11
00:00:34,850 --> 00:00:39,140
and twenty-one thousand tons of
diesel poured into the rivers.

12
00:00:39,810 --> 00:00:41,460
Was it the frozen ground?

13
00:00:41,810 --> 00:00:43,420
Hold on to that question.

14
00:00:43,770 --> 00:00:45,120
We'll come back to it.

15
00:00:46,315 --> 00:00:49,345
Frozen ground sounds
like a great foundation.

16
00:00:49,735 --> 00:00:51,345
It's as hard as concrete.

17
00:00:51,475 --> 00:00:52,625
But there's a catch.

18
00:00:53,255 --> 00:00:56,215
The moment you build a
warm house on top of it,

19
00:00:56,255 --> 00:00:58,445
the ice underneath starts to melt.

20
00:00:58,795 --> 00:01:02,885
And a house standing on melting
ice does exactly what you'd expect.

21
00:01:03,115 --> 00:01:03,685
It sinks.

22
00:01:03,955 --> 00:01:04,675
It tilts.

23
00:01:04,715 --> 00:01:05,325
It cracks.

24
00:01:06,518 --> 00:01:08,548
So how does Norilsk do it?

25
00:01:08,598 --> 00:01:11,388
How do you keep a whole
city standing on ice?

26
00:01:11,618 --> 00:01:14,148
And what happens when
the ice starts losing?

27
00:01:14,498 --> 00:01:16,568
To answer that, we need to go down.

28
00:01:17,098 --> 00:01:18,628
About ten meters down.

29
00:01:23,407 --> 00:01:28,507
Norilsk sits on the edge of the
Taymyr Peninsula in Arctic Siberia,

30
00:01:28,547 --> 00:01:31,157
at sixty-nine degrees north.

31
00:01:31,647 --> 00:01:35,437
It is the largest city this
far north on the planet.

32
00:01:36,087 --> 00:01:39,197
Murmansk, in Russia's
northwest, is bigger,

33
00:01:39,347 --> 00:01:41,077
but it lies further south.

34
00:01:42,540 --> 00:01:43,950
The climate is brutal.

35
00:01:44,280 --> 00:01:49,330
The average temperature over a whole year
is around minus eight degrees Celsius.

36
00:01:49,960 --> 00:01:53,490
In January, the average is
almost minus twenty-seven.

37
00:01:53,900 --> 00:01:56,350
The record low is minus fifty-three.

38
00:01:56,940 --> 00:02:01,060
And for about six weeks every winter, from
the end of November until the middle of

39
00:02:01,100 --> 00:02:03,890
January, the sun does not rise at all.

40
00:02:04,316 --> 00:02:06,986
Nobody would build a
city here for the view.

41
00:02:07,436 --> 00:02:10,606
Norilsk exists because of
what lies under the snow.

42
00:02:11,196 --> 00:02:14,606
The ground here holds one of
the richest deposits of nickel,

43
00:02:14,736 --> 00:02:16,786
copper and palladium on Earth.

44
00:02:17,256 --> 00:02:19,936
Today, the company that
mines it, Nornickel,

45
00:02:19,976 --> 00:02:23,066
produces more than forty percent
of the world's palladium,

46
00:02:23,216 --> 00:02:26,286
a metal used in car exhaust catalysts.

47
00:02:26,754 --> 00:02:28,644
The city's beginnings are dark.

48
00:02:28,974 --> 00:02:32,634
In nineteen thirty-five, the Soviet
government opened a labor camp here called

49
00:02:32,674 --> 00:02:33,524
Norillag.

50
00:02:33,914 --> 00:02:37,314
Over the next twenty-one years, hundreds
of thousands of prisoners were sent to dig

51
00:02:37,354 --> 00:02:40,104
the mines and build the
first factories and streets.

52
00:02:40,674 --> 00:02:44,464
Historians estimate around four hundred
thousand people passed through the camp.

53
00:02:45,134 --> 00:02:47,884
Official records list more
than sixteen thousand deaths,

54
00:02:47,994 --> 00:02:50,284
and the real number is
believed to be higher.

55
00:02:50,764 --> 00:02:55,694
Those prisoners were the first to discover
the problem we're talking about today.

56
00:02:56,084 --> 00:02:59,034
Because when they built
their first stone houses,

57
00:02:59,224 --> 00:03:02,874
the ground underneath
simply refused to cooperate.

58
00:03:03,424 --> 00:03:07,314
To see why, we need to know
what permafrost really is.

59
00:03:10,305 --> 00:03:12,955
Permafrost isn't a special kind of ice.

60
00:03:13,405 --> 00:03:14,335
It's a definition.

61
00:03:14,865 --> 00:03:20,845
Any ground that stays at or below zero
degrees Celsius for at least two years in a

62
00:03:20,885 --> 00:03:22,995
row counts as permafrost.

63
00:03:23,405 --> 00:03:26,655
It can be rock, sand, clay or peat.

64
00:03:26,925 --> 00:03:28,635
What matters is the temperature.

65
00:03:30,278 --> 00:03:33,788
The permafrost around
Norilsk is continuous.

66
00:03:34,278 --> 00:03:36,248
That means it isn't patchy.

67
00:03:36,378 --> 00:03:41,528
It's everywhere, under every street,
every factory and every apartment block.

68
00:03:42,772 --> 00:03:45,902
But the very top of the
ground behaves differently.

69
00:03:46,332 --> 00:03:51,022
Every summer, the upper layer thaws,
and every winter it freezes again.

70
00:03:51,972 --> 00:03:53,922
Scientists call it the active layer.

71
00:03:54,292 --> 00:03:57,422
Near Norilsk, it is about one meter thick.

72
00:03:57,752 --> 00:04:01,302
Below it, the ground stays
frozen all year round.

73
00:04:02,551 --> 00:04:06,061
Here's why that frozen
layer matters so much.

74
00:04:06,371 --> 00:04:09,261
In permafrost, ice works like glue.

75
00:04:09,651 --> 00:04:14,701
It fills the gaps between grains of
sand and clay and locks them together.

76
00:04:15,271 --> 00:04:18,021
Frozen clay can carry enormous weight.

77
00:04:18,511 --> 00:04:23,061
Thawed clay, full of melted water,
turns into something closer to mud.

78
00:04:24,256 --> 00:04:26,346
Now, put a building on top of it.

79
00:04:26,416 --> 00:04:30,436
A heated building, in a place where it's
minus thirty outside and plus twenty

80
00:04:30,476 --> 00:04:31,206
inside.

81
00:04:31,716 --> 00:04:34,426
Heat flows down through
the floor, year after year.

82
00:04:34,596 --> 00:04:37,106
The ground under the building
warms, the ice melts,

83
00:04:37,196 --> 00:04:39,006
and the soil loses its strength.

84
00:04:39,336 --> 00:04:41,566
One side settles faster than the other.

85
00:04:41,736 --> 00:04:42,766
The walls crack.

86
00:04:42,956 --> 00:04:47,026
That is exactly what happened to
the first stone houses of Norilsk.

87
00:04:49,152 --> 00:04:52,242
Between nineteen thirty-seven
and nineteen forty,

88
00:04:52,712 --> 00:04:58,342
around thirty low stone houses were
built in Norilsk on shallow foundations,

89
00:04:58,712 --> 00:05:01,402
the way you'd build almost anywhere else.

90
00:05:02,252 --> 00:05:04,502
According to Russian engineering studies,

91
00:05:04,612 --> 00:05:08,202
about half of them began to
deform almost immediately.

92
00:05:09,521 --> 00:05:12,551
The ground was doing
what frozen ground does.

93
00:05:12,741 --> 00:05:15,591
As soon as it was warmed
from above, it gave way.

94
00:05:16,081 --> 00:05:20,501
For the next fifteen years or so, builders
tried to solve this by digging down to

95
00:05:20,541 --> 00:05:24,631
solid rock and standing buildings
on columns that reached it.

96
00:05:24,901 --> 00:05:27,901
That works, but only where
rock is close to the surface,

97
00:05:27,941 --> 00:05:29,991
and it's slow and expensive.

98
00:05:30,845 --> 00:05:34,535
A city of tens of thousands
couldn't grow like that.

99
00:05:34,945 --> 00:05:37,875
Soviet engineers needed a different idea.

100
00:05:38,045 --> 00:05:41,855
And the idea they settled
on sounds almost backwards.

101
00:05:42,285 --> 00:05:46,215
Instead of fighting the frozen
ground, they decided to protect it.

102
00:05:46,625 --> 00:05:49,445
And the way they did it
is hiding in plain sight,

103
00:05:49,485 --> 00:05:51,555
under every building in the city.

104
00:05:54,454 --> 00:05:59,404
Soviet building codes describe two
ways of building on permafrost.

105
00:05:59,994 --> 00:06:04,824
Principle Two accepts that the ground
will thaw and tries to design for it.

106
00:06:05,254 --> 00:06:07,124
Principle One does the opposite.

107
00:06:07,494 --> 00:06:11,704
It says: keep the ground frozen
for the whole life of the building.

108
00:06:12,214 --> 00:06:15,244
Most of Norilsk was
built on Principle One.

109
00:06:16,308 --> 00:06:17,658
Here's how it works.

110
00:06:18,208 --> 00:06:22,458
First, long piles are driven or
drilled deep into the permafrost,

111
00:06:22,568 --> 00:06:24,218
well below the active layer.

112
00:06:24,648 --> 00:06:28,818
As the ground around them refreezes,
the piles become frozen in place,

113
00:06:29,028 --> 00:06:31,278
gripped by the ice along
their whole length.

114
00:06:31,588 --> 00:06:33,928
Think of a stick frozen into an ice cube:

115
00:06:33,968 --> 00:06:35,158
you can't pull it out.

116
00:06:35,468 --> 00:06:38,498
The building's weight is
carried by that frozen grip.

117
00:06:39,034 --> 00:06:43,584
Second, and this is the clever part,
the building doesn't touch the ground.

118
00:06:44,074 --> 00:06:49,374
It stands on those piles above an open,
ventilated gap between the soil and the

119
00:06:49,414 --> 00:06:50,364
first floor.

120
00:06:50,934 --> 00:06:54,094
Look at almost any old
apartment block in Norilsk,

121
00:06:54,134 --> 00:06:58,204
and you'll see it: the whole building
seems to hover above the snow.

122
00:06:58,984 --> 00:07:01,094
That gap is the real foundation.

123
00:07:01,624 --> 00:07:05,194
It turns the space under the house
into a fridge for the ground.

124
00:07:05,604 --> 00:07:09,664
In winter, freezing air blows through
it and pulls heat out of the ground,

125
00:07:09,704 --> 00:07:12,114
so the permafrost gets even colder.

126
00:07:12,564 --> 00:07:16,234
In summer, the building's shadow
keeps the sun off the soil.

127
00:07:16,624 --> 00:07:20,754
The heat from the apartments escapes
into the air instead of into the ground.

128
00:07:21,363 --> 00:07:25,553
It's the same reason you'll see
something else everywhere in the city:

129
00:07:26,003 --> 00:07:27,813
pipes running above ground.

130
00:07:28,303 --> 00:07:33,723
Hot water and heating pipes buried in the
soil would act like a giant heater under

131
00:07:33,763 --> 00:07:34,513
the streets.

132
00:07:35,103 --> 00:07:39,213
So they're lifted up, wrapped, and
kept away from the frozen ground.

133
00:07:40,607 --> 00:07:42,837
Put together, it's a simple deal.

134
00:07:43,287 --> 00:07:48,537
The city keeps the ground frozen, and
the frozen ground holds the city up.

135
00:07:48,947 --> 00:07:53,127
It worked well enough that Norilsk
grew into a city of panel blocks,

136
00:07:53,167 --> 00:07:58,477
five and nine storeys high, most of
them built before the nineteen eighties.

137
00:07:58,894 --> 00:08:01,164
But this deal has a weak point.

138
00:08:01,514 --> 00:08:04,324
It only works if the ground stays cold.

139
00:08:04,774 --> 00:08:07,834
And in Norilsk, the ground
has been getting warmer,

140
00:08:07,874 --> 00:08:10,084
from two directions at once.

141
00:08:10,514 --> 00:08:12,804
One of them comes from the city itself.

142
00:08:16,046 --> 00:08:18,396
The first problem is the city itself.

143
00:08:18,786 --> 00:08:22,356
That ventilated gap only works
if air can move through it.

144
00:08:22,646 --> 00:08:27,026
When snow piles up against the building,
it acts like a blanket and keeps the ground

145
00:08:27,066 --> 00:08:27,716
warm.

146
00:08:28,126 --> 00:08:32,806
When the gap gets blocked, or when a
leaking hot-water or sewage pipe floods the

147
00:08:32,846 --> 00:08:36,876
space under a building, the ice
below starts to melt from the top.

148
00:08:37,457 --> 00:08:41,057
Researchers who measured ground temperatures
in Norilsk found that at ten meters

149
00:08:41,097 --> 00:08:45,357
deep, the permafrost used to be between
minus seven and minus half a degree before

150
00:08:45,397 --> 00:08:46,907
mass construction began.

151
00:08:47,337 --> 00:08:49,227
By the two thousands,
in parts of the city,

152
00:08:49,417 --> 00:08:52,107
it was between minus two and
a half and plus half a degree.

153
00:08:52,437 --> 00:08:55,897
In some spots, the ground had warmed by
three to five degrees compared with the

154
00:08:55,937 --> 00:08:57,127
nineteen forties.

155
00:08:57,477 --> 00:08:59,627
Some pockets weren't
frozen at all anymore.

156
00:09:00,208 --> 00:09:02,238
The second problem is the climate.

157
00:09:02,848 --> 00:09:05,448
Between the nineteen
seventies and the twenty tens,

158
00:09:05,488 --> 00:09:09,898
the average air temperature in Norilsk
rose by one point four degrees.

159
00:09:10,528 --> 00:09:14,798
That's a lot for a place where the whole
system depends on staying below zero.

160
00:09:15,228 --> 00:09:20,028
And the Arctic as a whole has been warming
almost four times faster than the rest of

161
00:09:20,068 --> 00:09:22,058
the planet since nineteen seventy-nine.

162
00:09:22,854 --> 00:09:25,704
When frozen ground warms,
even before it melts,

163
00:09:25,774 --> 00:09:26,624
it gets weaker.

164
00:09:26,974 --> 00:09:29,004
The ice grip on the piles loosens.

165
00:09:29,574 --> 00:09:34,094
A study by American and Russian scientists
estimated that for Norilsk buildings from

166
00:09:34,134 --> 00:09:38,374
the nineteen sixties, the ground's ability
to carry their weight had already dropped

167
00:09:38,414 --> 00:09:40,844
by about fifteen percent
by twenty thirteen.

168
00:09:41,334 --> 00:09:43,714
For buildings from the
seventies and eighties,

169
00:09:43,754 --> 00:09:45,524
by about twenty-one percent.

170
00:09:45,937 --> 00:09:48,867
The same team modeled what comes next.

171
00:09:49,097 --> 00:09:52,547
By twenty twenty, they projected
a drop of more than a third,

172
00:09:52,737 --> 00:09:57,347
larger than the safety margins most
of those buildings were designed with.

173
00:09:57,637 --> 00:10:02,467
By twenty fifty, older foundations could
lose around two thirds of their strength.

174
00:10:02,817 --> 00:10:06,247
These are model estimates with
a wide range of uncertainty,

175
00:10:06,497 --> 00:10:08,327
but they all point the same way.

176
00:10:08,714 --> 00:10:10,764
And the damage is not just on paper.

177
00:10:11,314 --> 00:10:15,244
By the mid twenty tens, around three hundred
structures in Norilsk had significant

178
00:10:15,674 --> 00:10:16,304
deformations.

179
00:10:16,694 --> 00:10:20,204
More than a hundred residential buildings
were in a state of structural failure,

180
00:10:20,374 --> 00:10:23,204
and about five thousand families
were still living in them.

181
00:10:25,494 --> 00:10:26,914
And almost fifty buildings,
five and nine storeys tall,

182
00:10:26,954 --> 00:10:30,324
built from the sixties to the
eighties, had already been taken apart.

183
00:10:30,740 --> 00:10:32,610
You may have seen a scarier number online:

184
00:10:32,940 --> 00:10:35,590
that sixty percent of the
buildings in Norilsk are deformed.

185
00:10:35,960 --> 00:10:39,730
That figure comes from a single statement
by a regional emergency official in twenty

186
00:10:39,770 --> 00:10:42,310
sixteen, and nobody
published how it was counted.

187
00:10:42,880 --> 00:10:45,910
Permafrost scientists who surveyed
the city gave a lower figure:

188
00:10:46,200 --> 00:10:50,190
around thirty-five percent of buildings in
Norilsk and Talnakh showing deformation.

189
00:10:50,840 --> 00:10:52,430
Lower, but still about one in three.

190
00:10:52,680 --> 00:10:54,790
And then, in twenty
twenty, came the fuel tank.

191
00:10:57,075 --> 00:10:59,465
On the twenty-ninth of May, twenty twenty,

192
00:10:59,855 --> 00:11:03,805
a huge fuel tank at a power
plant in Norilsk failed.

193
00:11:04,415 --> 00:11:07,795
About twenty-one thousand
tons of diesel poured out,

194
00:11:07,835 --> 00:11:10,385
into the soil and into local rivers.

195
00:11:11,015 --> 00:11:15,145
It was one of the worst oil spills
ever recorded in the Arctic.

196
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:18,510
At first, the company
that owned the plant,

197
00:11:18,620 --> 00:11:22,350
a subsidiary of Nornickel,
pointed to the permafrost.

198
00:11:22,840 --> 00:11:27,590
The supports under the tank, it said,
had sunk because the ground had thawed.

199
00:11:27,920 --> 00:11:30,480
That story made headlines
around the world:

200
00:11:30,520 --> 00:11:34,910
the first big disaster
caused by melting permafrost.

201
00:11:36,151 --> 00:11:40,441
But the investigations told
a more complicated story.

202
00:11:40,851 --> 00:11:46,071
An engineering review commissioned by
Nornickel found that some of the tank's piles

203
00:11:46,111 --> 00:11:50,401
were shorter than designed and had
never been set into the bedrock.

204
00:11:51,511 --> 00:11:55,001
Russia's industrial safety
regulator went further.

205
00:11:56,351 --> 00:12:00,031
It found no sign of thawed
permafrost under the tank,

206
00:12:00,071 --> 00:12:04,551
and blamed weak foundations and
missing inspections instead.

207
00:12:04,591 --> 00:12:10,341
The tank had been out of service for
repairs and had gone back into use in twenty

208
00:12:10,381 --> 00:12:12,881
nineteen without being properly checked.

209
00:12:13,434 --> 00:12:18,544
In twenty twenty-one, a Russian court
ordered Nornickel to pay one hundred and

210
00:12:18,584 --> 00:12:23,314
forty-six billion rubles,
nearly two billion US dollars,

211
00:12:23,354 --> 00:12:25,484
for the environmental damage.

212
00:12:26,074 --> 00:12:28,664
The company paid it in March of that year.

213
00:12:28,994 --> 00:12:33,284
It was the largest environmental
fine in Russia's history.

214
00:12:33,722 --> 00:12:36,292
So was it the permafrost,
or was it people?

215
00:12:36,602 --> 00:12:39,312
The honest answer is that it
doesn't really matter which.

216
00:12:39,862 --> 00:12:43,712
A building on frozen ground only
stands as long as two things hold:

217
00:12:43,922 --> 00:12:46,712
the ground stays cold,
and humans do their job.

218
00:12:46,922 --> 00:12:48,812
In Norilsk, both are under pressure.

219
00:12:49,122 --> 00:12:51,832
So what can a city do when
its ground is getting warmer?

220
00:12:52,082 --> 00:12:55,412
It can fight back, with a pipe
that needs no power at all.

221
00:12:57,574 --> 00:13:00,424
If the ground is getting
too warm on its own,

222
00:13:00,514 --> 00:13:02,524
the answer is to cool it down.

223
00:13:02,994 --> 00:13:06,274
And engineers have a device
that does exactly that,

224
00:13:06,314 --> 00:13:08,604
with no electricity at all.

225
00:13:08,874 --> 00:13:13,554
It's called a thermosiphon, and it's one
of the smartest bits of engineering you've

226
00:13:13,594 --> 00:13:14,584
never noticed.

227
00:13:15,943 --> 00:13:18,543
It's a sealed metal pipe,
pushed deep into the ground,

228
00:13:18,583 --> 00:13:22,513
with a little liquid inside,
often ammonia or carbon dioxide.

229
00:13:23,163 --> 00:13:25,653
In winter, the top of the
pipe is in freezing air.

230
00:13:25,983 --> 00:13:28,223
The liquid at the bottom
picks up heat from the soil,

231
00:13:28,263 --> 00:13:29,953
turns into vapor and rises.

232
00:13:30,383 --> 00:13:33,183
At the cold top, the vapor
gives its heat away to the air,

233
00:13:33,223 --> 00:13:35,643
turns back into liquid,
and runs down again.

234
00:13:35,683 --> 00:13:38,113
Over and over, all winter long.

235
00:13:38,540 --> 00:13:41,670
In summer, when the air
is warmer than the ground,

236
00:13:41,860 --> 00:13:43,630
the cycle simply stops.

237
00:13:44,240 --> 00:13:47,770
So the pipe only ever moves
heat in one direction:

238
00:13:47,940 --> 00:13:48,850
out of the ground.

239
00:13:49,160 --> 00:13:53,390
Every winter, it stores up
extra cold for the summer ahead.

240
00:13:54,757 --> 00:13:58,487
You've probably seen
thermosiphons without knowing it.

241
00:13:58,697 --> 00:14:03,897
The Trans-Alaska Pipeline is held up by
more than a hundred and twenty thousand of

242
00:14:03,937 --> 00:14:04,327
them.

243
00:14:04,917 --> 00:14:10,357
The Qinghai–Tibet railway, which crosses
about five hundred and fifty kilometers of

244
00:14:10,397 --> 00:14:15,877
permafrost, uses them together with
embankments of crushed rock that let cold air

245
00:14:15,917 --> 00:14:16,387
in.

246
00:14:16,804 --> 00:14:19,214
Norilsk is now doing the same.

247
00:14:19,764 --> 00:14:24,074
After pilot projects on two apartment
buildings in twenty nineteen and twenty

248
00:14:24,114 --> 00:14:29,694
twenty, the authorities began installing
soil cooling systems under more homes.

249
00:14:30,184 --> 00:14:34,664
In twenty twenty-four, Russia's ministry
for the Far East and the Arctic said

250
00:14:34,704 --> 00:14:38,654
hundreds of houses in Norilsk would
get them by twenty thirty-five.

251
00:14:39,367 --> 00:14:42,377
At the same time, the city
is being partly rebuilt.

252
00:14:42,887 --> 00:14:46,447
Under a renovation program agreed in twenty
twenty-one between the government and

253
00:14:46,487 --> 00:14:50,687
Nornickel, dozens of failing buildings
are planned to be demolished and replaced

254
00:14:50,727 --> 00:14:52,157
with new apartment blocks.

255
00:14:52,747 --> 00:14:56,607
And Nornickel has put around a hundred and
sixty-five buildings and structures under

256
00:14:56,647 --> 00:15:01,457
monitoring, with sensors that track the
ground temperature and every tiny movement.

257
00:15:03,717 --> 00:15:07,767
So, why don't the houses in
Norilsk sink into the permafrost?

258
00:15:08,137 --> 00:15:10,947
Because the city was built
on a promise to the ground:

259
00:15:11,037 --> 00:15:12,447
we will keep you frozen.

260
00:15:13,217 --> 00:15:17,427
Stilts lift the buildings into the
wind, pipes stay above the surface,

261
00:15:17,617 --> 00:15:22,237
and now, more and more, steel tubes quietly
pump the heat out of the earth every

262
00:15:22,277 --> 00:15:22,787
winter.

263
00:15:23,528 --> 00:15:24,898
But here's the twist.

264
00:15:25,248 --> 00:15:28,338
Norilsk doesn't really
stand on frozen ground.

265
00:15:28,548 --> 00:15:30,938
It stands on ground it keeps frozen.

266
00:15:31,468 --> 00:15:35,978
The city works like a giant
refrigerator turned upside down,

267
00:15:36,048 --> 00:15:38,458
cooling the earth beneath its own feet.

268
00:15:38,848 --> 00:15:43,398
And the warmer the Arctic gets, the
harder that refrigerator has to work.

269
00:15:44,153 --> 00:15:45,843
Norilsk isn't alone.

270
00:15:46,853 --> 00:15:48,803
Scientists estimate that by twenty fifty,

271
00:15:49,173 --> 00:15:53,243
nearly four million people and around
seventy percent of the buildings,

272
00:15:53,573 --> 00:15:58,713
roads and pipelines in the Arctic's
permafrost zone will sit in areas where the

273
00:15:58,753 --> 00:16:00,583
ground is likely to thaw.

274
00:16:01,113 --> 00:16:04,583
Norilsk is just where the
problem shows up first.

275
00:16:05,165 --> 00:16:11,555
So tonight, somewhere in Norilsk, it's
minus thirty outside and plus twenty inside.

276
00:16:11,965 --> 00:16:15,665
And ten meters below the floor,
the ground is still frozen,

277
00:16:15,705 --> 00:16:19,235
because the whole city is
working to keep it that way.

278
00:16:20,524 --> 00:16:22,834
So here's my question for you.

279
00:16:23,064 --> 00:16:27,844
Would you live in a city where the ground
under your house has to be refrigerated to

280
00:16:27,884 --> 00:16:28,994
keep it standing?

281
00:16:29,444 --> 00:16:34,374
Tell me in the comments, and tell me which
place on Earth we should explain next.

282
00:16:34,704 --> 00:16:36,214
This was How It's Run.
